  "question": {
    "text": "In an increasing geometric progression of positive terms, the sum of the second and sixth terms is $\\frac{70}{3}$ and the product of the third and fifth terms is 49 . Then the sum of the $4^{\\text {th }}, 6^{\\text {th }}$ and $8^{\\text {th }}$ terms is equal to :"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "96",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "91",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "84",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "78",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "B"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "$\\mathrm{T}_2+\\mathrm{T}_6=\\frac{70}{3}$\n$\\mathrm{ar}+\\mathrm{ar}^5=\\frac{70}{3}$\n$\\mathrm{~T}_3 \\cdot \\mathrm{T}_5=49$\n$\\mathrm{ar}^2 \\cdot \\mathrm{ar}^4=49$\n$\\mathrm{a}^2 \\mathrm{r}^6=49$\n$\\mathrm{ar}^3=+7, \\mathrm{a}=\\frac{7}{\\mathrm{r}^3}$\n$\\mathrm{ar}\\left(1+\\mathrm{r}^4\\right)=\\frac{70}{3}$\n$\\frac{7}{\\mathrm{r}^2}\\left(1+\\mathrm{r}^4\\right)=\\frac{70}{3}, \\mathrm{r}^2=\\mathrm{t}$\n$\\frac{1}{\\mathrm{t}}\\left(1+\\mathrm{t}^2\\right)=\\frac{10}{3}$\n$3 \\mathrm{t}^2-10 \\mathrm{t}+3=0$\n$\\mathrm{t}=3, \\frac{1}{3}$\nIncreasing G.P. \n$\\mathrm{r}^2=3, \\mathrm{r}=\\sqrt{3}$\n$\\mathrm{T}_4+\\mathrm{T}_6+\\mathrm{T}_8$\n$=\\mathrm{ar}^3+\\mathrm{ar}^5+\\mathrm{ar}^7$\n$=\\operatorname{ar}^3\\left(1+\\mathrm{r}^2+\\mathrm{r}^4\\right)$\n$=7(1+3+9)=91$"
  }
